<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dulo clan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dulo clan was the ruling house of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>, recorded in the Nominalia of the Bulgarian Khans. Its members included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a>, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a> in the 7th century, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparuh</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>. The clan ruled Danubian Bulgaria until the mid-8th century, when rival clans displaced it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dulo-clan.jpg" alt="Dulo clan" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mpb eu · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khans_Dulo_of_Bulgaria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Dulo were a ruling dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>, who were of Turkic origin. Their elite is generally considered related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>, and the clan is said to descend from the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>, a centralised monarchy, unlike earlier Hunno-Turkic entities, which were tribal confederations. Its exact origin is obscure. Some researchers link it to the five Duolu tribes of the Western Turks, and Omeljan Pritsak connected its name with that of an old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> ruling house, though Peter B. Golden regards the Turkic and Xiongnu associations as speculative. Etymologies of the name are also speculative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Most of what is known comes from the Nominalia of the Bulgarian khans. It lists Avitohol, who lived 300 years, and Irnik, who lived 150, as Dulo, and rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> traced descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> through Irnik, possibly Attila's attested son Ernak. Historians disagree. Josef Marquart identified Avitohol with Attila, while Jean W. Sedlar thought the Attila connection doubtful. Vasil Zlatarski held that Irnik and Ernak were different persons, since the Nominalia gives Irnik's accession as 437, before Attila's death in 453.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a>, a member of the clan, revolted against the Pannonian Avars and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. In the second half of the 7th century his sons split the family and spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: Bezmer in Ukraine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kotrag">Kotrag</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuver">Kuber</a> in Balkan Macedonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and Alcek at Sepino, Bojano and Isernia. Bezmer was the last Dulo ruler north of the Danube, and Asparukh the first south of it, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tervel">Tervel</a> and then Sevar, the last Dulo ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes</a>, in 761 or 762 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> rose up, killed their hereditary lords and set up Teletzes as king, which historians usually read as a massacre of the Dulo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In the pagan period succession in the clan followed traditions brought from the Eurasian steppe, including the rulers' divine ancestry. The Khan stood at the head of the clan as head of state, military leader and probably high priest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangra">Tangra</a>. Mercia MacDermott claimed that the clan held the dog sacred and that a Bulgarian expression, he kills the dog, meaning he gives the orders, recalls a Dulo khan sacrificing a dog to Tangra for the whole community.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dulo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 630s Kubrat of the <b>Dulo clan</b> threw off the overlordship of the Avars and united the Onogur and Bulgar tribes of the steppe between the Kuban and the lower Dnieper.</blockquote><a href="/state/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
